<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayeux</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augustodurum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bayeux is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvados">Calvados</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Normandy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> coast. As Augustodurum it was the Roman chief town of the Gallic Baiocasses, whose name it later took, and it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat. The Normans gained the area in 924, and the town is famous for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry</a>, kept there, and as the first French town liberated after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normandy-landings">Normandy landings</a> in 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayeux.jpg" alt="Bayeux" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mairie de Bayeux · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayeux_centre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
